asnappar_346	Asnappar	\N	Asnappar	\N	Male	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Old Testament	f	\N	\N	Probably the same as Assur-bani-pal (Sardanapalos of the Greeks), styled the “great and noble” (Ezra 4:10), was the son and successor (B.C. 668) of Esar-haddon (q.v.). He was “luxurious, ambitious, and cruel, but a magnificent patron of literature.” He formed at Nineveh a library of clay tablets, numbering about 10,000. These are now mostly in the British Museum. They throw much light on the history and antiquities of Assyria.	Easton's Bible Dictionary	Ezra 4:10	1	recKDjSXCDhbqcySA	\N	Person
